insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes) select 'how-to-choose-a-reliable-service-provider', 'How to Choose a Reliable Service Provider in South Africa', 'A practical checklist for hiring plumbers, electricians, mechanics and other service providers — from asking the right questions to getting quotes in writing.', $md$Whether your geyser has burst, your car is making a strange noise or you need someone to repaint the house, choosing the right service provider can save you money, time and a great deal of stress. The wrong choice can mean poor workmanship, hidden costs or a job that has to be redone.

This guide gives you a simple, repeatable process you can use for almost any service.

## 1. Be clear about what you need

Before you contact anyone, write down the problem or the job in a few sentences. Include:

- What needs to be done, and where.
- When you need it done, and whether it is urgent.
- Any photos that show the problem.
- Your rough budget, if you have one.

A clear brief helps businesses give you accurate quotes, and it makes it easier to compare them fairly.

## 2. Shortlist at least three businesses

Search for the service in your area and create a shortlist of two or three providers. Look at each business profile carefully:

- **Is the information complete?** Businesses that take the time to describe their services, list operating hours and add real photos of their work are usually more organised.
- **Do they serve your area?** Check the location and service areas so you don't pay unnecessary call-out fees.
- **Do they offer the specific service you need?** A general builder may not be the best choice for specialised waterproofing, for example.

## 3. Check qualifications and registrations

Many trades and professions in South Africa have formal registration requirements. Ask for proof where it applies to your job. For example:

- Electrical work on fixed installations should be done by a registered electrical contractor, who can issue a Certificate of Compliance (CoC) where required.
- Plumbers installing or replacing geysers should be able to provide the relevant plumbing certificate of compliance.
- Health professionals are generally registered with the relevant statutory council, such as the HPCSA.
- Building work on new homes may involve NHBRC requirements for the builder.

You don't need to be an expert in every regulation. Simply asking "Which body are you registered with, and can I see your certificate?" tells you a lot about how a business operates.

## 4. Ask the right questions

When you speak to each business, ask:

1. How long have you been doing this type of work?
2. Can you show me examples of similar jobs?
3. What exactly is included in the price, and what isn't?
4. Is there a call-out or assessment fee?
5. How long will the job take, and when can you start?
6. Do you offer a workmanship guarantee? For how long?
7. How do you prefer to be paid, and when?

Pay attention to how clearly and patiently they answer. Good communication before the job usually means good communication during it.

## 5. Always get a written quote

A verbal estimate is easy to forget or dispute. Ask for a written quote — even a WhatsApp message or email is better than nothing — that lists:

- The work to be done, item by item.
- Materials and their cost.
- Labour cost.
- VAT, if the business is VAT-registered.
- Payment terms and the expected completion date.

Compare quotes line by line rather than only looking at the total. A very low quote can mean lower-quality materials or work that has been left out.

## 6. Be careful with deposits

Some businesses reasonably ask for a deposit to buy materials. Protect yourself by:

- Keeping deposits proportionate to the cost of materials.
- Paying into a business bank account rather than cash where possible.
- Getting a written acknowledgement of every payment.
- Never paying the full amount before the work starts.

If someone pressures you to pay everything upfront "today only", treat it as a warning sign.

## 7. Watch for red flags

Be cautious if a provider:

- Refuses to give a written quote.
- Cannot give a physical address or a consistent contact number.
- Arrives without the tools or materials they said they'd bring.
- Pressures you to make a decision immediately.
- Asks you to pay into a personal account that doesn't match the business name.

## 8. Keep a record of everything

Save quotes, messages, invoices, certificates and photos of the work before and after. If something goes wrong, a clear paper trail makes it much easier to resolve the issue with the business directly or, if necessary, to approach the relevant ombud or the National Consumer Commission.

## Putting it into practice

Choosing a service provider doesn't need to be complicated. Define the job, compare a few well-presented businesses, ask direct questions, get everything in writing and pay sensibly. On Findora you can compare business profiles, services and operating hours, then call, WhatsApp or send an enquiry directly — so you stay in control of the conversation from the start.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '36 days', 'How to Choose a Reliable Service Provider in South Africa', 'A practical checklist for hiring plumbers, electricians, mechanics and other service providers — from asking the right questions to getting quotes in writing.', 4 from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'finding-services' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes) select 'starting-a-small-business-in-south-africa', 'Starting a Small Business in South Africa: A Step-by-Step Guide', 'From testing your idea to registering with CIPC and SARS, opening a business bank account and finding your first customers — a practical roadmap for new entrepreneurs.', $md$Starting a business is one of the most rewarding things you can do, and South Africa has no shortage of entrepreneurs solving real problems in their communities. The first few months, however, can feel overwhelming. This guide breaks the process into clear steps so you know what to do first, what can wait, and where to get reliable information.

*This article is general guidance, not legal or tax advice. Requirements change, so confirm details with the relevant authority or a qualified professional.*

## Step 1: Test your idea before you spend money

The most expensive mistake new businesses make is building something nobody wants to pay for. Before registering anything:

- **Talk to potential customers.** Ask what they currently do to solve the problem and what frustrates them about it.
- **Check the competition.** Search your area for similar businesses. Competition is not bad — it proves there is demand — but you need a reason for customers to choose you.
- **Make a small first sale.** Offer your service to a few people, even at a discount, and learn from the experience.

## Step 2: Choose a business structure

The most common structures for small businesses are:

- **Sole proprietor.** You trade in your own name or a trading name. It is simple and inexpensive, but there is no legal separation between you and the business.
- **Private company — (Pty) Ltd.** Registered with the Companies and Intellectual Property Commission (CIPC). The company is a separate legal entity, which can make it easier to open accounts, tender for work and bring in partners.
- **Non-profit company (NPC)** for organisations whose purpose is public benefit rather than profit.

Many entrepreneurs start as sole proprietors and register a company once the business grows. An accountant can help you decide which structure suits your situation.

## Step 3: Register with CIPC (if you choose a company)

Company registration is done online through CIPC. You will typically:

1. Reserve a company name (or register with a number-based name).
2. Complete the registration with the details of the directors.
3. Receive your registration certificate and company registration number.

Keep your registration documents safe — banks, clients and suppliers will ask for them. Remember that companies must also file annual returns with CIPC.

## Step 4: Get your tax affairs in order

Every business has tax obligations. Registered companies are generally registered for income tax with the South African Revenue Service (SARS). Sole proprietors declare business income on their personal tax return.

Other registrations may apply depending on your turnover and whether you employ people, for example VAT registration once you reach the compulsory threshold, and PAYE, UIF and SDL when you have employees. A registered tax practitioner can help you understand what applies to you.

## Step 5: Open a separate business bank account

Mixing personal and business money makes it very hard to know whether you are actually making a profit, and it creates headaches at tax time. Open a business account as soon as possible and:

- Pay all business income into it.
- Pay all business expenses from it.
- Pay yourself a regular, fixed amount rather than withdrawing money whenever you need it.

## Step 6: Understand the rules for your industry

Some sectors have specific registration or licensing requirements — for example food businesses, health professionals, financial advisors, security providers, electricians and builders. Find out early whether your trade needs a licence, certificate or professional registration, and budget for it.

## Step 7: Price for profit, not just for sales

Work out what it actually costs you to deliver your product or service, including:

- Materials or stock.
- Transport and fuel.
- Data, airtime and equipment.
- Your own time.

Then add a profit margin. Many new businesses underprice to win customers and end up working hard for very little. It is easier to offer a discount later than to raise prices for existing customers.

## Step 8: Make your business easy to find

Customers can't support a business they can't find. At minimum:

- Create a professional business profile on directories such as Findora, with a clear description, your services and real photos.
- Set up a WhatsApp Business account with your operating hours and a catalogue.
- Ask satisfied customers to recommend you to friends and family.

## Step 9: Keep simple records from day one

Record every sale and expense, keep invoices and receipts, and reconcile your bank account every month. Even a spreadsheet works. Good records help you make decisions, apply for funding and stay compliant.

## Step 10: Look for support

South Africa has many support programmes for small businesses, including government agencies, enterprise development programmes run by larger companies, incubators and local business chambers. Speak to other entrepreneurs in your area too — they often know which programmes are genuinely useful.

## The bottom line

Start small, test your idea, register properly, separate your finances and make it easy for customers to reach you. Every established business you admire started with the same first steps.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '33 days', 'Starting a Small Business in South Africa: A Step-by-Step Guide', 'From testing your idea to registering with CIPC and SARS, opening a business bank account and finding your first customers — a practical roadmap for new entrepreneurs.', 4 from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'starting-a-business' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes) select 'low-cost-marketing-ideas-for-small-businesses', '12 Low-Cost Marketing Ideas for South African Small Businesses', 'You don''t need a big budget to attract customers. These practical marketing ideas cost little or nothing and work especially well for local service businesses.', $md$Marketing doesn't have to mean expensive billboards or radio adverts. For most small and local businesses, the most effective marketing is simple, consistent and personal. Here are twelve ideas you can start using this week.

## 1. Make your business easy to find online

When people need a service, they search for it. Make sure your business appears with complete, accurate information on free platforms:

- A detailed Findora business profile with your services, photos and contact details.
- Your Google Business Profile, so you appear on Google Maps.
- A WhatsApp Business profile with your hours, address and catalogue.

Use the same business name, phone number and address everywhere. Consistency builds trust with both customers and search engines.

## 2. Ask happy customers for referrals

Word of mouth remains the most powerful form of marketing in South Africa. After a job goes well, simply ask: "If you know anyone else who needs this service, would you mind passing on my number?" Many people are happy to help — they just need to be asked.

## 3. Show your work with real photos

Before-and-after photos are especially powerful for trades such as building, painting, landscaping, cleaning and beauty. Take clear photos in good light, ask clients for permission if their home or face is visible, and add the best ones to your business profile and social media.

## 4. Use WhatsApp Status wisely

Your contacts check WhatsApp Status every day. Post a few times a week with:

- A completed job.
- A short tip related to your service.
- Your availability for the coming week.

Keep it useful. Constant sales messages will make people mute you.

## 5. Partner with complementary businesses

Find businesses that serve the same customers but don't compete with you. A plumber and a tiler, a wedding photographer and a florist, or a mechanic and a panel beater can refer customers to each other. Agree on a simple arrangement and keep each other's business cards handy.

## 6. Get involved in your community

Sponsor a prize at a school fundraiser, offer a free workshop at your local library, or support a community clean-up. Local involvement builds recognition and goodwill that money can't easily buy.

## 7. Create helpful content

Answer the questions customers ask you every week. A mechanic could post "Five signs your brakes need attention"; an accountant could explain important tax deadlines. Helpful content positions you as an expert and gives people a reason to follow you.

## 8. Make your contact details impossible to miss

Put your phone number and WhatsApp number on your vehicle, uniform, invoices, quotes and email signature. Every interaction is a chance to be remembered.

## 9. Respond quickly

Speed wins business. Many customers contact two or three providers and choose whoever replies first with a clear answer. Set aside specific times each day to respond to calls, WhatsApps and enquiries, and use quick-reply templates for common questions.

## 10. Offer a simple loyalty incentive

A small discount on the next service, a free add-on or a "refer a friend" thank-you can turn one-off customers into regulars. Keep the offer simple and easy to explain.

## 11. Use local networks

Neighbourhood WhatsApp and community groups can be valuable, but follow each group's rules. Introduce yourself once, then contribute helpfully rather than posting adverts repeatedly.

## 12. Measure what works

Ask every new customer: "How did you hear about us?" Keep a simple tally. On Findora, your dashboard shows how many people viewed your profile and how many called, sent a WhatsApp message or submitted an enquiry. After a few months you'll know exactly where to focus your time.

## Start with three

You don't need to do everything at once. Pick three ideas that suit your business, do them consistently for three months, and review the results. Small, steady efforts compound into a reliable flow of customers.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '30 days', '12 Low-Cost Marketing Ideas for South African Small Businesses', 'You don''t need a big budget to attract customers. These practical marketing ideas cost little or nothing and work especially well for local service businesses.', 3 from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'business-growth' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes) select 'why-local-business-discovery-matters', 'Why Local Business Discovery Matters for South African Communities', 'When customers can easily find the businesses around them, money circulates locally, jobs are created and communities become more resilient. Here''s why discovery matters.', $md$Every town and suburb in South Africa is full of capable businesses: the mechanic who has kept the neighbourhood's cars running for twenty years, the young graphic designer working from home, the family-run bakery, the tutor helping matric learners after school. Yet many residents never find them. They default to large chains or to whatever appears first online, even when an excellent local option is a few streets away.

Making local businesses easier to discover is not just convenient. It has real economic and social benefits.

## Money that stays local

When you spend money with a local business, a larger share of that money tends to stay in your community. The owner pays local staff, buys from local suppliers and spends their own income nearby. That spending supports other businesses in turn.

Large national businesses play an important role, but a healthy local economy needs a strong base of small and medium enterprises as well.

## Small businesses create jobs

Small, medium and micro enterprises are a major source of employment in South Africa. When a local business gets more customers, it is often in a position to hire an assistant, take on an apprentice or offer extra shifts. Helping customers find these businesses is one practical way to support job creation.

## The visibility gap

Many small businesses rely entirely on word of mouth. That works well within existing networks, but it makes it hard to reach new customers — people who have just moved to the area, younger customers who search on their phones, or people in neighbouring towns.

Common reasons small businesses stay invisible include:

- No website, or an outdated one.
- Information scattered across social media posts that are hard to search.
- Contact numbers that change or are only shared privately.
- No photos or descriptions of services.

A structured business profile solves many of these problems. It gives the business one clear place to describe what it does, where it operates and how to get in touch.

## Better choices for customers

Customers benefit from discovery too. When you can compare several local providers — their services, prices where published, operating hours and photos — you make better decisions. You may find someone closer, more specialised or more affordable than the first name that came to mind.

Direct contact matters as well. Being able to call or WhatsApp a business straight away means faster answers and a personal relationship from the start.

## Resilience in difficult times

Communities with diverse local businesses tend to cope better with economic shocks. If one large employer closes, a network of small enterprises can help absorb the impact. Local businesses also often adapt quickly — offering deliveries, changing products or serving new needs — when circumstances change.

## Township and rural economies

Township and rural businesses face particular visibility challenges, from inconsistent addresses to limited marketing budgets. Yet they serve millions of customers and represent enormous entrepreneurial energy. Making these businesses searchable — by service, by town and by category — helps them reach customers beyond their immediate streets.

## What you can do

Supporting local businesses doesn't require a big commitment:

1. **Search locally first.** Before defaulting to a familiar brand, see who offers the service near you.
2. **Recommend good businesses.** Tell friends, share profiles and give honest feedback directly to the business.
3. **Encourage businesses to list themselves.** If your favourite local shop or tradesperson isn't easy to find online, let them know about free listing options.
4. **Be a good customer.** Pay on time, communicate clearly and respect agreed arrangements.

## A shared opportunity

Findora was created to make it easier for South Africans to find and connect with the businesses around them, and for those businesses to be seen. Every listing, every search and every enquiry is a small contribution to stronger local economies.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '27 days', 'Why Local Business Discovery Matters for South African Communities', 'When customers can easily find the businesses around them, money circulates locally, jobs are created and communities become more resilient. Here''s why discovery matters.', 3 from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'local-economy' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes) select 'building-your-digital-visibility', 'Digital Visibility for Small Businesses: Where to Start', 'You don''t need an expensive website to be found online. Learn the foundations of digital visibility — consistent information, business profiles, search basics and social media.', $md$"Digital visibility" simply means how easily customers can find your business when they look online. For many small businesses, it is the difference between a phone that rings and one that stays quiet. The good news is that you can build a solid online presence without a big budget or technical skills.

## Start with the foundation: consistent information

Before anything else, decide on your official business details and use them everywhere:

- **Business name** — exactly the same spelling each time.
- **Phone and WhatsApp numbers** — ideally ones that won't change.
- **Address or service area** — where you are based and which areas you cover.
- **Operating hours** — including weekends and public holidays.

Search engines compare information across different websites. When your details match everywhere, they are more confident that your business is real and current. Customers trust consistency too.

## Create your core business profiles

You don't need to be everywhere. Start with a few high-value platforms:

1. **A business directory profile**, such as Findora, with a full description, services, prices where appropriate, photos and contact details. Each approved profile gets its own web page that can appear in search results.
2. **A Google Business Profile**, so you appear on Google Maps and in local search results.
3. **WhatsApp Business**, with a business description, hours, catalogue and quick replies.

Complete every field you can. A half-finished profile suggests a half-finished business.

## Write descriptions customers understand

Describe your business in the words your customers use. Instead of "comprehensive automotive solutions", write "car services, diagnostics, brake repairs and wheel alignment in Klerksdorp". Mention:

- What you do.
- Who you serve.
- Where you operate.
- What makes you different — experience, specialisation, guarantees or response times.

These plain-language descriptions also help search engines understand when to show your business.

## Use real photos

Photos build trust faster than words. Add:

- Your logo and a clear cover photo.
- Your premises, vehicle or workspace.
- Examples of completed work.
- Your team, if they are comfortable with it.

Avoid stock photos that could belong to any business. Customers want to see you.

## Understand the basics of search

You don't need to become an SEO expert, but a few principles help:

- **Relevance** — your profile should clearly describe the services people search for.
- **Location** — mention the towns and suburbs you serve.
- **Completeness** — full profiles generally perform better than sparse ones.
- **Freshness** — update your information when something changes.

Avoid keyword stuffing (repeating the same phrase unnaturally). Write for people first.

## Social media: choose one or two platforms

Pick the platforms your customers actually use. For many local businesses, Facebook and Instagram work well for visual services, while TikTok can suit businesses that can create short, practical videos. LinkedIn is useful for professional and business-to-business services.

Post consistently rather than frequently. Two good posts a week beats ten rushed ones followed by months of silence.

## Make contacting you effortless

Visibility is only valuable if people can reach you easily:

- Add click-to-call and WhatsApp links wherever possible.
- Reply to messages and enquiries quickly.
- Keep your voicemail professional and check it daily.

## Track and improve

Look at the numbers regularly. On Findora, your dashboard shows profile views, phone clicks, WhatsApp clicks, website clicks and enquiries over time. If views are high but contacts are low, improve your description, photos or pricing information. If views are low, check your category, location and service list.

## A simple 30-day plan

- **Week 1:** Decide your official details and complete your Findora and Google profiles.
- **Week 2:** Add at least six good photos and list your main services.
- **Week 3:** Set up WhatsApp Business and share your profile link with existing customers.
- **Week 4:** Review your first results and update anything that's unclear.

Digital visibility is built step by step. Start with the foundation, keep it accurate and improve it gradually.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '24 days', 'Digital Visibility for Small Businesses: Where to Start', 'You don''t need an expensive website to be found online. Learn the foundations of digital visibility — consistent information, business profiles, search basics and social ', 3 from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'business-growth' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes) select 'customer-communication-tips', 'Customer Communication That Wins Business: Practical Tips for Service Providers', 'How you respond to enquiries, quote, update and follow up often matters as much as the work itself. Practical communication habits that turn enquiries into loyal customers.', $md$Two businesses can do equally good work, yet one is always busy while the other struggles. Very often, the difference is communication. Customers remember how you made them feel: whether you replied promptly, explained things clearly and kept your promises.

Here are practical habits that make a real difference.

## Respond quickly — even if it's a short reply

When a customer sends an enquiry, they are often contacting several businesses at once. The first clear, helpful reply has a big advantage.

If you can't give a full answer straight away, acknowledge the message:

> "Thanks for your message, Thandi. I'm on a job until 3pm and will call you this afternoon to discuss your geyser."

That single message keeps the customer from moving on.

## Ask good questions before you quote

Avoid quoting blindly. Ask what you need to know:

- What exactly is the problem or requirement?
- Where is the job, and is there parking or access?
- Is there a deadline?
- Can they send photos?

Good questions show expertise and reduce surprises later.

## Make quotes easy to understand

A clear quote should include:

- A description of the work.
- Itemised materials and labour.
- What is not included.
- Payment terms and validity period.
- Your business details and contact number.

Use plain language. If something is technical, add a sentence explaining why it is needed.

## Set expectations — and then meet them

Tell customers when you'll arrive, how long the job will take and what will happen next. If something changes, tell them before they have to ask. A message such as "We're running 30 minutes late because of traffic on the N12" is far better than an unexplained delay.

## Keep customers updated during longer jobs

For work that takes several days, send short progress updates with a photo. It reassures the customer, reduces anxious phone calls and documents your work.

## Handle complaints calmly

Even excellent businesses get complaints. When it happens:

1. **Listen fully** without interrupting.
2. **Acknowledge** the customer's frustration.
3. **Investigate** the facts.
4. **Offer a fair solution** and agree on next steps.
5. **Follow up** to confirm the issue is resolved.

A complaint handled well can create a more loyal customer than a job with no problems at all.

## Use the right channel

- **Phone calls** are best for urgent or complex discussions.
- **WhatsApp** is convenient for quick updates, photos and confirmations.
- **Email** suits formal quotes, invoices and records.

Whatever you use, confirm important agreements in writing.

## Be professional on WhatsApp

WhatsApp feels casual, but customers still judge professionalism:

- Use a WhatsApp Business account with your business name and hours.
- Greet customers by name and sign off clearly.
- Avoid voice notes for important details such as prices or dates — they're hard to refer back to.
- Respect reasonable hours unless the customer has an emergency.

## Respect privacy

Customers trust you with their names, phone numbers and addresses. Use that information only for the purpose it was shared — to respond to the enquiry and provide the service. Don't add customers to marketing lists without permission, and store records securely. This is good practice and aligns with the Protection of Personal Information Act (POPIA).

## Follow up after the job

A short message a few days later — "Just checking that everything is still working well" — shows you care and often leads to referrals. It is also the right moment to ask whether they'd recommend you to others.

## Create a few templates

Save time with ready-made messages for common situations: acknowledging an enquiry, sending a quote, confirming a booking, and following up. Personalise each one with the customer's name and details.

## In summary

Reply fast, ask good questions, quote clearly, keep people informed and follow up. These habits cost nothing, yet they consistently turn enquiries into customers — and customers into ambassadors for your business.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '21 days', 'Customer Communication That Wins Business: Practical Tips for Service ', 'How you respond to enquiries, quote, update and follow up often matters as much as the work itself. Practical communication habits that turn enquiries into loyal customer', 3 from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'business-growth' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes) select 'choosing-professional-services', 'Choosing an Accountant, Attorney or Consultant: What to Look For', 'Professional services involve trust, confidentiality and expertise. How to evaluate qualifications, fees, communication and fit before you appoint an accountant, attorney or consultant.', $md$Hiring a professional — an accountant, attorney, financial advisor, architect or business consultant — is different from hiring a tradesperson. You are paying for expertise and judgement, often on matters that are confidential or have long-term consequences. A good choice can protect you from costly mistakes; a poor one can create them.

Here is how to make a confident decision.

## Define the problem you need solved

"I need an accountant" is a starting point, but be more specific:

- Do you need monthly bookkeeping, annual financial statements, tax returns, or payroll?
- Is your legal matter a property transfer, a labour dispute, a contract review or an estate?
- Are you looking for a once-off project or an ongoing relationship?

Professionals often specialise. A clear description helps you find the right specialist and get a more accurate fee estimate.

## Check registrations and professional membership

Many professions in South Africa are regulated or have recognised professional bodies. Before appointing someone, ask which body they belong to and confirm their status where a public register is available. For example:

- Attorneys are admitted by the High Court and regulated by the Legal Practice Council.
- Tax practitioners must be registered with SARS and a recognised controlling body.
- Accountants may be members of professional bodies such as SAICA, SAIPA or others.
- Financial advisors should be authorised by, or represent a provider authorised by, the Financial Sector Conduct Authority (FSCA).

Membership doesn't guarantee a perfect fit, but it means the professional has met certain standards and is accountable to a regulatory or disciplinary process.

## Ask about relevant experience

Ask how often they handle matters like yours and whether they work with businesses or individuals similar to you. A conveyancer who processes many transfers in your area, or an accountant familiar with your industry, will usually work faster and anticipate issues better.

## Understand how fees work

Professional fees can be structured in different ways:

- **Hourly rates**, common for legal and consulting work.
- **Fixed fees** for clearly defined tasks, such as a tax return or a simple will.
- **Monthly retainers** for ongoing bookkeeping or advisory services.
- **Fees set by tariff or guideline** in certain regulated areas.

Ask for a written fee estimate or engagement letter that explains what is included, how extra work is charged and when payment is due. If a fee seems unusually low or high, ask why.

## Evaluate communication

During your first conversation, notice:

- Do they explain things in language you understand?
- Do they ask thoughtful questions about your situation?
- Do they tell you honestly what they can and cannot do?
- How quickly do they respond?

You will be working closely with this person. Clear communication and mutual respect matter.

## Confidentiality and data protection

Professionals handle sensitive personal and financial information. Ask how they store documents, who in their office will have access, and how they share files with you. Responsible firms take the Protection of Personal Information Act (POPIA) seriously and will be comfortable answering these questions.

## Watch for warning signs

Be cautious if a professional:

- Guarantees an outcome that depends on a court, regulator or market.
- Pressures you to sign immediately.
- Is vague about fees or refuses to put them in writing.
- Suggests ways to avoid legal obligations that sound too good to be true.
- Cannot confirm their registration.

## Start with a defined first task

If you are unsure, begin with a smaller, clearly defined piece of work. It is a low-risk way to experience how the professional works before committing to a longer relationship.

## Using Findora to compare professionals

On Findora you can browse categories such as Legal Services, Financial Services and Professional Services, filter by location, review services and descriptions, and contact providers directly by phone, WhatsApp or enquiry. Use the questions in this guide during that first conversation, and you'll be well placed to choose a professional you can trust.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '18 days', 'Choosing an Accountant, Attorney or Consultant: What to Look For', 'Professional services involve trust, confidentiality and expertise. How to evaluate qualifications, fees, communication and fit before you appoint an accountant, attorney', 3 from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'finding-services' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes) select 'technology-tools-for-small-businesses', 'Affordable Technology Tools Every Small Business Should Consider', 'The right tools save hours every week. A practical overview of affordable technology for communication, invoicing, bookings, payments, file storage and security.', $md$Technology should make running your business easier, not more complicated. You don't need expensive systems to work efficiently — many effective tools are free or cost less than a weekly fuel top-up. This guide covers the categories that matter most for small businesses and what to look for in each.

## 1. Business communication

**WhatsApp Business** is the starting point for many South African businesses. Useful features include:

- A business profile with your address, hours and website.
- Quick replies for common questions.
- Labels to organise customers (new enquiry, quoted, paid).
- A catalogue of products or services.

A **professional email address** using your own domain (for example info@yourbusiness.co.za) looks more credible than a free personal address and is inexpensive to set up with most hosting providers.

## 2. Quoting and invoicing

Handwritten invoices get lost and look unprofessional. Cloud accounting and invoicing tools let you:

- Create branded quotes and invoices in minutes.
- Track which invoices are unpaid.
- Send automatic payment reminders.
- Keep records ready for your accountant and for SARS.

Many offer free or low-cost plans for very small businesses. Choose one that your accountant is comfortable working with.

## 3. Bookings and scheduling

If you work by appointment — salons, consultants, tutors, health practitioners — an online booking tool can reduce back-and-forth messages. Look for:

- Automatic reminders to reduce no-shows.
- Calendar sync with your phone.
- The option to take deposits for certain services.

Even a shared digital calendar is a big step up from a paper diary.

## 4. Accepting payments

Customers increasingly expect to pay by card or instant EFT. Options include card machines that connect to your phone, payment links you can send by WhatsApp or email, and QR code payments. Compare transaction fees, settlement times and monthly costs before choosing.

## 5. File storage and backups

Store important documents — registration certificates, contracts, quotes, invoices and photos — in a cloud storage service rather than only on one phone or laptop. If a device is lost or stolen, your records are safe.

Organise files into simple folders (Clients, Suppliers, Finance, Compliance) and name files consistently so you can find them quickly.

## 6. Productivity and collaboration

If you work with a team, shared documents, spreadsheets and task lists help everyone stay on the same page. A simple shared list of jobs with statuses ("to quote", "booked", "in progress", "invoiced") can replace dozens of messages.

## 7. Online presence

Your online presence is also a technology tool:

- A complete Findora business profile that customers can find by service and location.
- A Google Business Profile for map searches.
- A simple website if your business benefits from more detailed information.

On Findora, your dashboard also shows real statistics — profile views, call and WhatsApp clicks, and enquiries — so you can see which efforts are working.

## 8. Cybersecurity basics

Small businesses are targets for scams and account takeovers. Protect yourself with a few habits:

- **Use strong, unique passwords** and a password manager.
- **Turn on two-factor authentication** for email, banking, social media and WhatsApp.
- **Be suspicious of urgent payment requests**, especially changed banking details sent by email. Phone the supplier on a known number to confirm.
- **Keep devices updated** with the latest software.
- **Back up regularly.**

## 9. Choosing tools wisely

Before signing up for anything, ask:

1. What problem does this solve, and how much time will it save?
2. What does it cost per month, including transaction fees?
3. Is it easy to cancel, and can I export my data?
4. Does it work well on a phone?
5. How does the provider protect my and my customers' data?

Start with one tool, use it properly for a month, then add the next.

## Final thought

The best technology is the technology you actually use. Choose simple tools that remove repetitive work, keep your records organised and help customers reach you — then spend the time you save on growing your business.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '15 days', 'Affordable Technology Tools Every Small Business Should Consider', 'The right tools save hours every week. A practical overview of affordable technology for communication, invoicing, bookings, payments, file storage and security.', 3 from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'business-technology' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes) select 'optimise-your-business-profile', 'How to Optimise Your Findora Business Profile for More Enquiries', 'A step-by-step guide to writing a strong description, choosing categories, listing services, adding photos and keeping your profile up to date so customers choose you.', $md$Your business profile often creates the first impression a customer has of your business. In a few seconds they decide whether to call you or move on to the next listing. This guide walks through each part of your Findora profile and how to make it work harder for you.

## 1. Choose the right category and subcategory

Customers often browse by category, and search results consider your category and subcategory. Choose the option that best describes your **main** service. If you offer several types of service, use the services section to cover the others — each service can be assigned its own service category.

## 2. Write a clear, specific description

Your description should answer four questions in the first few sentences:

1. **What do you do?**
2. **Who do you do it for?**
3. **Where do you operate?**
4. **Why should customers choose you?**

Compare these two descriptions:

> "We provide quality services at affordable prices. Contact us today."

> "Family-run plumbing business serving Klerksdorp, Orkney and Stilfontein since 2012. We install and repair geysers, fix leaks and unblock drains for homes and small businesses, with a 12-month workmanship guarantee."

The second one is specific, builds trust and includes words customers actually search for.

Keep paragraphs short, avoid capital letters for emphasis, and don't copy your description from another business.

## 3. Add a short tagline

The tagline appears near your name and in search results. Use it to state your speciality in one line, for example "Emergency electrical repairs in Mahikeng — 7 days a week."

## 4. List every important service

Services help customers understand exactly what you offer and help your profile appear in more searches. For each service:

- Use a clear name customers would recognise ("Wheel alignment", not "WA service").
- Add a one- or two-sentence description.
- Choose a price type: fixed, starting from, hourly, quotation required, or free.
- Add availability if relevant ("Weekdays, by appointment").

You don't have to publish prices. However, "starting from" prices help customers decide whether to contact you and reduce enquiries that don't fit your pricing.

## 5. Upload a logo and cover photo

Your logo appears in search results and on your profile, so make sure it is clear at a small size. Use a square image with a plain background where possible.

Your cover photo sits at the top of your profile. Choose a wide, high-quality image that represents your business — your shopfront, team, vehicle or a strong example of your work.

## 6. Build a gallery of real photos

Add photos that show:

- Completed work (before and after is ideal).
- Your premises or workspace.
- Equipment, products or vehicles.
- Your team at work.

Write a short description for each photo. Descriptions help customers who use screen readers and give search engines more context.

## 7. Complete your contact details and hours

Add every channel you actively monitor — phone, WhatsApp, email and website — and your social media links. Then set your operating hours. Customers are more likely to contact a business when they know it's open.

Only list numbers you answer. An unanswered phone can cost you more than a missing one.

## 8. Get your location right

Select your province and city, add your suburb, and list the other areas you serve. If you work from home, you can hide your street address while still appearing in searches for your area. Adding map coordinates enables the "Get Directions" button for customers who visit you.

## 9. Respond to enquiries quickly

When a customer submits an enquiry, you'll receive an email and see it in your dashboard. Fast, friendly responses dramatically increase your chances of winning the job. Mark enquiries as read and archive them when they're dealt with to keep your inbox manageable.

## 10. Review your statistics monthly

Your dashboard shows profile views, phone clicks, WhatsApp clicks, website clicks and enquiries. Use them to improve:

- **Few views?** Check your category, service names and location details.
- **Views but few contacts?** Improve your description, photos and pricing information.
- **Many calls but few enquiries?** That's fine — customers are reaching you directly.

## 11. Keep it current

Update your profile whenever your hours, services, prices or contact details change. An accurate profile builds trust; an outdated one frustrates customers.

## Quick checklist

- [ ] Correct category and subcategory
- [ ] Specific description and tagline
- [ ] All main services listed
- [ ] Logo, cover photo and at least six gallery photos
- [ ] Phone, WhatsApp and/or email
- [ ] Operating hours
- [ ] Location and service areas

A complete, honest and up-to-date profile is one of the most effective free marketing tools available to your business.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '12 days', 'How to Optimise Your Findora Business Profile for More Enquiries', 'A step-by-step guide to writing a strong description, choosing categories, listing services, adding photos and keeping your profile up to date so customers choose you.', 4 from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'business-growth' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes) select 'building-customer-trust', 'Building Customer Trust: Practical Steps for Small Businesses', 'Trust is the foundation of every sale. Learn how transparency, consistency, professionalism and honest handling of problems help customers feel confident choosing your business.', $md$People buy from businesses they trust. That's especially true for services, where customers often can't judge quality until the job is done. Large brands build trust with advertising budgets; small businesses build it through hundreds of small, consistent actions.

Here are practical ways to earn — and keep — your customers' confidence.

## Be transparent about who you are

Customers want to know they're dealing with a real, accountable business:

- Use your actual business name consistently.
- Show where you're based or which areas you serve.
- Introduce the people behind the business — even a short "about us" helps.
- If you're registered with CIPC, a professional body or an industry association, say so (and be ready to show proof).

On Findora you can choose to display your business registration number on your profile. Only claim registrations you actually hold.

## Be clear about prices

Uncertainty about cost is one of the biggest reasons customers hesitate. Where possible:

- Publish "starting from" prices or price ranges for common services.
- Explain call-out or assessment fees upfront.
- Provide written, itemised quotes.
- Tell customers immediately if the scope changes and the price will be affected — before doing the extra work.

## Show real evidence of your work

Photos of real jobs, examples of previous projects and honest descriptions of your experience are more convincing than claims like "best in town". Never use other businesses' photos or invent achievements.

## Communicate reliably

Trust grows when you do what you say:

- Arrive when you said you would, or let the customer know in advance if you'll be late.
- Return calls and messages promptly.
- Explain what you're doing and why.
- Confirm agreements in writing.

Small acts of reliability add up to a reputation.

## Handle mistakes honestly

Every business makes mistakes. What matters is how you respond. Acknowledge the problem, apologise sincerely, fix it properly and learn from it. Customers often become more loyal after seeing a problem handled well.

## Offer a guarantee you can honour

A workmanship guarantee — for example, returning to fix any defect in your work within a set period at no charge — reduces the customer's risk. Make the terms clear and realistic, and always honour them.

## Protect customer information

Customers share names, numbers, addresses and sometimes sensitive details with you. Treat that information with care:

- Use it only to provide the service and communicate about it.
- Don't share it with others without permission.
- Store it securely and delete what you no longer need.
- Don't send marketing messages unless customers have agreed.

Respecting privacy is both a legal obligation under POPIA and a powerful trust signal.

## Make it easy to reach you — before and after the sale

A business that disappears after receiving payment quickly loses trust. Keep the same contact numbers, respond to after-sales questions and be easy to find. A complete, up-to-date directory profile helps customers find you again when they need you.

## Be consistent across every channel

Your profile, WhatsApp, social media, vehicle branding and invoices should all show the same name, logo and contact details. Consistency signals stability and professionalism.

## Avoid shortcuts that damage trust

Some tactics may bring short-term attention but cause long-term harm:

- Fake reviews or testimonials.
- Misleading "verified" or award claims.
- Hidden fees.
- High-pressure sales tactics.
- Copying other businesses' content.

Findora doesn't allow fake reviews or misleading verification claims on listings, and verification badges are only shown after a real verification process.

## Trust is built over time

There's no shortcut to a trusted reputation, but every honest quote, punctual arrival and well-handled problem moves you closer. Customers notice — and they tell others.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '9 days', 'Building Customer Trust: Practical Steps for Small Businesses', 'Trust is the foundation of every sale. Learn how transparency, consistency, professionalism and honest handling of problems help customers feel confident choosing your bu', 3 from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'business-growth' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes) select 'supporting-local-economic-development', 'How Small Businesses Drive Local Economic Development', 'Small enterprises are central to jobs and opportunity in South African towns and townships. How local economic development works, and practical ways residents, businesses and organisations can strengthen it.', $md$Local economic development (LED) is about improving the economic well-being of a specific place — a town, a township, a district — by building on its own people, businesses and resources. In South Africa, municipalities have a role in creating an environment where local economies can grow, but much of the real activity happens through small businesses, entrepreneurs and the customers who support them.

## What local economic development looks like

Strong local economies tend to share some features:

- A variety of businesses, so the area isn't dependent on a single employer.
- Opportunities for local people to work, train and start enterprises.
- Infrastructure that supports trade — roads, electricity, connectivity and safe public spaces.
- Networks that connect businesses with customers, suppliers and each other.
- Money that circulates within the community rather than immediately leaving it.

## Why small businesses matter so much

Small, medium and micro enterprises (SMMEs) and informal businesses provide livelihoods for a large number of South Africans. They often:

- Employ people who live nearby.
- Buy from other local suppliers.
- Provide services that larger companies don't offer in smaller towns.
- Adapt quickly to local needs.
- Train young people through practical experience and apprenticeships.

When these businesses grow, the benefits spread through the community.

## The barriers small businesses face

Despite their importance, many small enterprises struggle with:

- **Visibility** — customers simply don't know they exist.
- **Access to finance** — limited credit history or collateral.
- **Access to markets** — difficulty reaching customers outside their immediate area or winning contracts from larger organisations.
- **Skills and information** — on compliance, marketing, finance or technology.
- **Costs** — transport, data, rent and electricity.

No single solution addresses all of these, but small improvements in each area add up.

## How residents can help

Individuals have more influence than they might think:

1. **Search for local options first** when you need a product or service.
2. **Pay promptly**, especially for small businesses with limited cash flow.
3. **Recommend good businesses** to friends, family and community groups.
4. **Give constructive feedback** directly to the business so it can improve.

## How businesses can help each other

- **Buy from each other.** A local caterer can use a local bakery; a construction company can hire a local electrician.
- **Refer customers** to trusted complementary businesses.
- **Share knowledge** through informal networks, chambers or WhatsApp groups.
- **Mentor newcomers** where you have experience to share.

## How larger organisations can help

Corporates, schools, churches, NGOs and government departments can make a big difference through local procurement — deliberately sourcing goods and services from businesses in the area — and through enterprise and supplier development programmes that offer training, mentorship and access to markets.

## The role of digital discovery

As more South Africans search for services on their phones, being discoverable online is becoming a basic requirement. Yet many capable local businesses remain invisible because they don't have a website or marketing budget.

Free business directories help close that gap. When a business can list its services, location, hours and contact details in one structured, searchable place, customers from across the town — and from neighbouring towns — can find it. Search data can also reveal unmet demand: services people search for but can't find locally, which may represent opportunities for new businesses.

## A shared responsibility

Local economic development isn't only a government programme. It happens every time a resident chooses a local provider, a business refers work to a neighbour or an organisation buys locally. Findora aims to make those choices easier by helping South Africans discover and connect with the businesses in their own communities.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '6 days', 'How Small Businesses Drive Local Economic Development', 'Small enterprises are central to jobs and opportunity in South African towns and townships. How local economic development works, and practical ways residents, businesses', 3 from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'local-economy' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.blog_posts (slug, title, excerpt, content, category_id, author_name, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, reading_minutes) select 'how-to-avoid-service-scams', 'How to Spot and Avoid Common Service Scams', 'Fake tradespeople, advance-fee tricks and changed banking details cost South Africans money every year. Learn the warning signs and simple habits that protect you.', $md$Most service providers are honest people trying to earn a living. Unfortunately, a minority of scammers take advantage of customers who need help quickly. Knowing the common tactics makes it much harder for them to succeed.

## Common scams to watch for

### The upfront payment scam

A "business" offers an unusually good price, then asks for full or large upfront payment — for materials, a "booking fee" or a "special discount that ends today". Once paid, they stop responding.

**Protect yourself:** Pay deposits only in proportion to genuine material costs, ideally directly to a supplier or into a business account that matches the business name. Never pay the full amount before work begins.

### Changed banking details

You receive an email or message, apparently from a business you've dealt with, saying their bank details have changed. The new account belongs to a fraudster.

**Protect yourself:** Always confirm changed banking details by phoning the business on a number you already know — not one supplied in the message.

### The fake listing or copied identity

Scammers copy the name, logo or photos of a real business and advertise under it with a different phone number.

**Protect yourself:** Check that contact details match across the business's profiles. Be cautious if a business's photos look like stock images or appear under different business names. On Findora, every listing is reviewed before publication, and you can report suspicious listings from any profile.

### The "unnecessary repairs" tactic

A provider inspects your geyser, car or roof and claims that extensive, urgent repairs are needed — far beyond the original problem.

**Protect yourself:** For large or unexpected repairs, ask for a written explanation and photos, then get a second quote before agreeing.

### Unrequested door-to-door services

Someone arrives unannounced offering to fix a problem they claim to have noticed, often demanding payment on the spot.

**Protect yourself:** Don't let unexpected visitors into your home. Take their details and verify the business independently before agreeing to anything.

### Overpayment and refund requests

A "customer" or "supplier" pays you more than agreed and asks you to refund the difference. The original payment later reverses.

**Protect yourself:** Wait for funds to clear fully in your account before refunding anything, and be suspicious of overpayments.

## General warning signs

Be cautious when someone:

- Pressures you to decide or pay immediately.
- Offers a price far below other quotes.
- Avoids giving a written quote, address or registration details.
- Insists on cash or untraceable payment methods.
- Uses a personal bank account in a different name.
- Gives inconsistent information about their business.

## Good habits that protect you

1. **Get at least two quotes** for significant work.
2. **Ask for proof** of relevant registrations and certificates.
3. **Get everything in writing** — quote, scope, timeline, payment terms.
4. **Keep records** of messages, invoices and payments.
5. **Pay in stages** linked to completed work.
6. **Trust your instincts.** If something feels wrong, pause.

## If something goes wrong

- Stop any further payments immediately.
- Contact your bank as soon as possible if you paid electronically.
- Report fraud to the South African Police Service and obtain a case number.
- Report the listing on the platform where you found the business — on Findora, use "Report this listing" on the business profile.
- For disputes with legitimate businesses, consider the relevant industry ombud or the National Consumer Commission.

## Staying safe while supporting local business

Scams shouldn't stop you from supporting local businesses. By comparing profiles, asking the right questions and paying sensibly, you can enjoy the benefits of local services while keeping your money safe. If you spot a suspicious listing on Findora, please report it — every report helps keep the platform trustworthy for everyone.
$md$, c.id, 'Findora Editorial Team', 'published', now() - interval '3 days', 'How to Spot and Avoid Common Service Scams', 'Fake tradespeople, advance-fee tricks and changed banking details cost South Africans money every year. Learn the warning signs and simple habits that protect you.', 3 from public.blog_categories c where c.slug = 'finding-services' on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.site_pages (slug, title, summary, content, seo_title, seo_description, is_published, show_in_footer) values ('about', 'About Findora', 'Findora is a South African business discovery platform that helps customers find trusted businesses and helps businesses be found.', $md$## Our purpose

South Africa is full of capable businesses — from family-run workshops and independent professionals to growing companies and township entrepreneurs. Too often, customers simply can't find them. Findora exists to close that gap.

We help customers **discover businesses, explore services and connect directly** with service providers across South Africa. And we help businesses of every size present themselves professionally and reach new customers — free of charge.

## What makes Findora different

- **Free business listings.** Any legitimate business can create a complete profile with services, photos, operating hours and contact details at no cost.
- **Direct connection.** Customers call, WhatsApp, email or send enquiries straight to the business. We don't take commission on the work you agree.
- **Reviewed listings.** Every listing is reviewed by our team before it is published, and anyone can report a listing that looks suspicious or inaccurate.
- **Honest information.** We don't publish fake reviews or invented statistics, and we only show verification badges after a real verification process.
- **Privacy by design.** We collect only what we need, never sell personal information, and give every user tools to export or delete their data.

## Who we serve

**Customers** looking for a plumber in Polokwane, a dentist in Durban, a caterer in Kimberley or an accountant in Cape Town can search by service, category and location and compare businesses side by side.

**Businesses** — sole proprietors, freelancers, small companies, non-profits and franchises — can showcase their services, receive enquiries and see real statistics on how customers interact with their profile.

## How we sustain the platform

Listings are free. In future, Findora may offer optional premium features and clearly labelled advertising or featured placements. Organic search results will always be ranked on relevance and useful information, and any paid placement will be clearly marked as featured or sponsored.

## Get in touch

We'd love to hear from you — whether you're a customer with feedback, a business owner with a question or an organisation interested in working with us. Visit our [contact page](/contact).
$md$, 'About Findora', 'Findora is a South African business discovery platform that helps customers find trusted businesses and helps businesses be found.', true, true) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.site_pages (slug, title, summary, content, seo_title, seo_description, is_published, show_in_footer) values ('advertising-policy', 'Advertising Policy', 'How advertising, featured and sponsored placements work on Findora, and the standards adverts must meet.', $md$Findora is free for customers and offers free business listings. To sustain the Platform, we may display advertising and offer featured or sponsored placements. This policy explains our principles.

## Our principles

1. **Clear labelling.** Advertisements are labelled "Advertisement". Paid placements of business listings are labelled "Featured" or "Sponsored".
2. **Organic results stay organic.** Normal search results are ranked by relevance and the usefulness of business information. Payment does not change a listing's organic ranking.
3. **No ads in private spaces.** We do not show advertisements on sign-in or registration pages, inside dashboards, in enquiry forms or in private communications.
4. **No disguised ads.** Ads are never designed to look like navigation, search results or business listings.
5. **Privacy first.** Personalised advertising cookies are used only with your consent. See our [Cookie Policy](/cookies).

## Third-party advertising

We may use Google AdSense to display ads. Google's ad content is subject to Google's own publisher and advertising policies. If you see an ad that seems inappropriate or misleading, please tell us through our [contact page](/contact).

## Direct advertising and sponsored placements

Businesses or organisations that advertise directly with Findora must ensure their adverts:

- Are truthful, not misleading and comply with South African law, including the Consumer Protection Act and applicable advertising codes.
- Don't promote prohibited goods or services listed in our [listing guidelines](/listing-guidelines).
- Don't contain offensive, discriminatory or sexually explicit content.
- Link to legitimate destinations that match the advert.

We may decline or remove any advertisement at our discretion.

## Featured listings

Featured listings may receive additional visibility, such as placement in a labelled "Featured listings" section. Featuring is not an endorsement or guarantee, and featured businesses must meet the same listing guidelines as all other businesses.

## Questions

For advertising enquiries, choose "Advertising" on our [contact form](/contact).
$md$, 'Advertising Policy', 'How advertising, featured and sponsored placements work on Findora, and the standards adverts must meet.', true, true) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.site_pages (slug, title, summary, content, seo_title, seo_description, is_published, show_in_footer) values ('contact', 'Contact Us', 'Questions, feedback or a problem with a listing? We''re here to help.', $md$Use the form to send us a message and choose the topic that fits best so it reaches the right person.

- **Problems with a listing?** It's quickest to use **Report this listing** on the business profile.
- **Privacy or POPIA requests** — such as access, correction or deletion of personal information — can be sent through this form (choose "Privacy / POPIA request") or emailed to our information officer.
- **Business owners:** many listing questions are answered in the [Help Centre](/help).
$md$, 'Contact Us', 'Questions, feedback or a problem with a listing? We''re here to help.', true, false) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.site_pages (slug, title, summary, content, seo_title, seo_description, is_published, show_in_footer) values ('cookies', 'Cookie Policy', 'What cookies Findora uses, why, and how to control them.', $md$Cookies are small text files stored in your browser. Findora uses as few cookies as possible and only uses non-essential cookies with your consent.

## Essential cookies (always on)

| Cookie | Purpose | Duration |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Authentication cookies (sb-…) | Keep you signed in securely and refresh your session | Session / up to the session lifetime set by our authentication provider |
| findora_consent | Remembers your cookie choices | 6 months |

These cookies are necessary for the Platform to work and cannot be switched off.

## First-party statistics (no cookies)

We count profile views, searches and contact-button clicks to give businesses statistics and improve Findora. These counts **do not use cookies**: they use a salted, anonymous identifier that changes every day and is not linked to your name or stored with your IP address.

## Analytics cookies (optional)

If you accept analytics cookies, we use Google Analytics 4 to understand how visitors use the Platform (for example which pages are visited). IP anonymisation is requested. Google may process this information outside South Africa.

## Advertising cookies (optional)

If advertising is enabled on the Platform and you accept advertising cookies, Google AdSense may use cookies to show personalised ads. If you don't accept, any ads shown are non-personalised. Advertisements are never shown on sign-in, registration, dashboard or enquiry screens.

## Managing your choices

You can change your choices at any time using **Cookie preferences** in the footer of every page. You can also delete or block cookies in your browser settings, although blocking essential cookies will prevent you from signing in.

For more about how we handle personal information, see our [Privacy Policy](/privacy).
$md$, 'Cookie Policy', 'What cookies Findora uses, why, and how to control them.', true, true) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.site_pages (slug, title, summary, content, seo_title, seo_description, is_published, show_in_footer) values ('help', 'Help Centre', 'Answers and guidance for customers and businesses using Findora.', $md$## Getting started

- **Customers** can search and contact businesses without an account. Create a free account to save businesses and track your enquiries.
- **Businesses** can [create a free listing](/list-your-business). Listings are reviewed before publication.

## Account help

- **Didn't receive your verification email?** Check your spam folder, then request a new link from the verification page or log-in screen.
- **Forgot your password?** Use [Forgot password](/forgot-password) to receive a secure reset link.
- **Want to change your details?** Go to Dashboard → Account Settings.
- **Want a copy of your data?** Go to Dashboard → Account Settings → Download my data.
- **Want to delete your account?** Go to Dashboard → Account Settings → Delete account. This also removes listings you are the only owner of.

## Listing help

- **Editing your listing:** Dashboard → My Businesses → select your business. Changes to published listings appear immediately.
- **Why is my listing pending?** New listings are reviewed, usually within two working days.
- **My listing was rejected:** the email and your dashboard explain why. Update the listing and resubmit it.
- **Adding photos:** use JPG, PNG or WebP images up to 4 MB. Images are resized and optimised automatically, and location metadata is removed for your privacy.
- **Removing your listing:** you can unpublish or delete it from the listing's management page at any time.

## Enquiries

- Enquiries sent through Findora are delivered to the business by email and appear in its dashboard. The business replies to you directly.
- Findora is not a party to agreements between customers and businesses. Always confirm prices, qualifications and terms before paying.

## Safety

Read our guide on [how to spot and avoid service scams](/blog/how-to-avoid-service-scams). If a listing looks suspicious, use **Report this listing** on the profile.

## Still need help?

[Contact us](/contact) and we'll respond as soon as possible, usually within two working days.
$md$, 'Help Centre', 'Answers and guidance for customers and businesses using Findora.', true, false) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.site_pages (slug, title, summary, content, seo_title, seo_description, is_published, show_in_footer) values ('how-it-works', 'How Findora Works', 'Find the right business, compare your options and connect directly — or list your own business for free.', $md$## For customers

### 1. Search or browse

Use the search bar to look for a business name, a service (such as "geyser installation" or "wheel alignment") or a keyword. You can also browse by [category](/categories) and narrow results by province, town, business type, price range and weekend availability.

You don't need an account to search, view profiles or contact businesses.

### 2. Compare businesses

Each business profile shows:

- A description of the business and its category.
- Services, with prices where the business has chosen to publish them.
- Operating hours, location and the areas served.
- Photos of the business and its work.
- Contact options and social media links.

Featured or sponsored listings, when offered, are always clearly labelled. Normal search results are ranked by relevance and the quality of the business information.

### 3. Connect directly

Choose how you'd like to get in touch:

- **Call Now** to speak to the business immediately.
- **WhatsApp** to send a message.
- **Send Enquiry** to submit a written request through Findora. The business receives it by email and in its dashboard, and replies to you directly.
- **Visit Website** or **Get Directions** where available.

### 4. Keep track (optional)

Create a free customer account to save favourite businesses and see the history of the enquiries you've sent.

## For businesses

### 1. Create your free account

[Register your business](/register/business) with your name, email and a password, and verify your email address.

### 2. Build your profile

Our step-by-step setup guides you through your business information, location, contact details and operating hours, branding and photos, and services and prices. Your progress is saved after each step.

### 3. Submit for review

When your profile is complete, submit it for publication. Our team checks every listing against our [listing guidelines](/listing-guidelines) — usually within two working days — and emails you the outcome.

### 4. Receive enquiries and grow

Once approved, your profile has its own web page that customers can find on Findora and through search engines. Your dashboard shows enquiries and real statistics such as profile views and calls, WhatsApp and website clicks, so you can see what's working.

## Our commitments

- Listings are free during our launch phase, with no commission on your work.
- We never publish fake reviews, ratings or statistics.
- We protect personal information in line with POPIA.
- Anyone can [report a listing](/report) that looks suspicious or incorrect.
$md$, 'How Findora Works', 'Find the right business, compare your options and connect directly — or list your own business for free.', true, false) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.site_pages (slug, title, summary, content, seo_title, seo_description, is_published, show_in_footer) values ('list-your-business', 'List Your Business on Findora — Free', 'Create a professional business profile, get found by customers across South Africa and receive enquiries directly. No listing fees and no commission.', $md$## What you get with a free listing

- **Your own profile page** with a search-engine-friendly web address, such as findora.co.za/businesses/your-business-name-town.
- **Services and prices** — list everything you offer, with fixed, "starting from", hourly or quotation-only pricing.
- **Photos** — your logo, a cover photo and a gallery of your work.
- **Contact buttons** — Call Now, WhatsApp, email, website and directions.
- **Enquiry inbox** — customers can send written enquiries that arrive by email and in your dashboard.
- **Real analytics** — see profile views, phone and WhatsApp clicks, website clicks and enquiries over time.
- **Full control** — edit your information whenever you like, or unpublish and delete your listing at any time.

## How to create your listing

1. **Register** with your name, email and phone number, then verify your email.
2. **Add your business details** — name, description, category and business type.
3. **Set your location** — province, town and the areas you serve.
4. **Add contact details and hours** — and confirm you're authorised to publish them.
5. **Upload your branding** — logo, cover photo and gallery.
6. **List your services** — with descriptions and prices if you choose.
7. **Review** your profile preview.
8. **Submit** for approval. We'll email you when it's live.

Most businesses finish in 10–15 minutes. Your progress is saved after each step.

## Who can list

Any legitimate business, professional or organisation operating in South Africa — sole proprietors, freelancers, private companies, partnerships, non-profits and franchises. Your business doesn't need to be CIPC-registered to list, but all information must be accurate. Please read our [listing guidelines](/listing-guidelines) before you start.

## Tips for a profile that attracts enquiries

- Write a specific description: what you do, who you serve, where, and why customers should choose you.
- Add at least six real photos of your work.
- List your main services with clear names.
- Keep your hours and contact details up to date.
- Reply to enquiries quickly.

Read our full guide: [How to optimise your Findora business profile](/blog/optimise-your-business-profile).
$md$, 'List Your Business on Findora — Free', 'Create a professional business profile, get found by customers across South Africa and receive enquiries directly. No listing fees and no commission.', true, false) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.site_pages (slug, title, summary, content, seo_title, seo_description, is_published, show_in_footer) values ('listing-guidelines', 'Business Listing Guidelines', 'The standards every Findora listing must meet. They keep the directory accurate, safe and useful for everyone.', $md$Every listing is reviewed before it is published. Listings that don't meet these guidelines may be rejected, edited, hidden, suspended or removed. We may update these guidelines from time to time.

## Who may list

- Legitimate businesses, professionals and organisations operating in South Africa.
- The person creating the listing must own the business or be authorised to represent it.
- Each physical business or distinct service business should have one listing. Duplicate listings will be merged or removed.

## Accurate information

- Use your real business name — no added keywords, slogans or locations in the name field.
- Describe your business honestly and specifically.
- Choose the category that best describes your main service.
- Only list services you actually provide.
- Prices, if shown, must be genuine and must include VAT if you are VAT-registered and charge it.
- Keep your contact details, hours and location up to date.

## Contact details and personal information

- Only publish contact details you are authorised to share publicly.
- If you work from home, you may hide your street address.
- Do not publish other people's personal information without their permission.

## Photographs

Photos must:

- Be your own, or you must have permission to use them.
- Accurately represent your business, premises, products or work.
- Not contain nudity, violence, offensive material or personal information of others without consent.
- Not be misleading stock images presented as your own work.

## Registrations and claims

- Only mention registrations, accreditations, awards or memberships you actually hold. We may ask for proof.
- Do not claim to be "verified by Findora" unless your listing shows a verification badge. Badges are added only by our team after a verification process.
- Do not publish fake reviews, testimonials or ratings.

## Prohibited listings

We do not publish listings for:

- Illegal goods or services, or businesses requiring a licence they don't hold.
- Weapons, drugs or controlled substances (other than lawfully registered pharmacies and medical practices).
- Adult sexual services.
- Gambling services that are not licensed in South Africa.
- Pyramid schemes, get-rich-quick offers or unregistered financial services.
- Hate speech, discrimination or content that incites harm.
- Listings intended to impersonate another business.

## Regulated professions

If your profession is regulated — for example health professionals, legal practitioners, financial advisors, security service providers, electricians or builders — you are responsible for complying with your regulator's rules, including any rules on advertising.

## Enquiries and customer data

Use customer enquiry details only to respond to that enquiry and provide your service, in line with the Protection of Personal Information Act (POPIA). Do not add customers to marketing lists without their consent.

## Reporting and takedowns

Anyone can [report a listing](/report). We review reports and may contact the business, request corrections, hide content or remove the listing. If you believe your listing was removed in error, please [contact us](/contact).
$md$, 'Business Listing Guidelines', 'The standards every Findora listing must meet. They keep the directory accurate, safe and useful for everyone.', true, true) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.site_pages (slug, title, summary, content, seo_title, seo_description, is_published, show_in_footer) values ('privacy', 'Privacy Policy', 'How Findora collects, uses, shares and protects personal information, and your rights under the Protection of Personal Information Act (POPIA).', $md$**Responsible party:** [Findora operating entity name] (Registration number: [number]), trading as Findora ("Findora", "we", "us").
**Information Officer:** [Name], [privacy email address].
**Physical address:** [address].

This policy explains how we process personal information when you use findora.co.za and related services (the "Platform"). We process personal information in accordance with the Protection of Personal Information Act 4 of 2013 (POPIA).

## 1. Information we collect

**Information you give us**

- **Account information:** name, email address, phone number (optional for customers), password (stored securely in hashed form by our authentication provider) and your account type.
- **Profile information you choose to add:** profile photo, city, province and a short bio.
- **Business listing information:** business name, description, category, registration number (optional), address and service areas, contact details, operating hours, services, prices, photographs and social media links. Listings are intended to be public.
- **Enquiries:** your name, email, optional phone number, subject and message when you contact a business through the Platform.
- **Reports and contact messages:** information you include when reporting a listing or contacting us.
- **Consent records:** when you accept our terms, opt in to marketing, consent to cookies or consent to the publication of business contact details.

**Information collected automatically**

- **Essential cookies** needed to keep you signed in and secure.
- **Privacy-conscious usage statistics:** counts of profile views, searches and contact-button clicks. These are linked to a salted, daily-rotating anonymous identifier, not to your name, and we do not store your IP address with them.
- **Analytics and advertising cookies** (for example Google Analytics or Google AdSense) only if you consent through our cookie preferences.
- **Server and security logs** used to protect the Platform against abuse.

## 2. Why we use personal information

We process personal information to:

1. Create and manage your account and authenticate you.
2. Publish business listings that businesses submit for publication.
3. Deliver your enquiries to the business you contact and send you a confirmation.
4. Send service emails, such as verification, password reset, listing decisions and enquiry notifications.
5. Moderate listings, investigate reports and prevent fraud, spam and abuse.
6. Provide businesses with aggregated statistics about their profiles.
7. Improve the Platform using aggregated or anonymised information.
8. Send marketing communications, only where you have opted in.
9. Comply with legal obligations.

Our lawful grounds include your consent, the performance of our agreement with you, our legitimate interests in operating a safe and useful directory, and compliance with the law.

## 3. Sharing personal information

- **With businesses you contact:** your enquiry details are shared with the business you choose to contact, so that it can reply to you.
- **Public listings:** business information submitted for publication is visible to the public. Businesses must confirm they are authorised to publish contact details.
- **Service providers (operators)** who process information on our behalf under appropriate agreements, including hosting (Vercel), database, authentication and file storage (Supabase), transactional email (Resend) and, where enabled and consented to, analytics and advertising services (Google).
- **Legal requirements:** where required by law or to protect rights, safety or property.

We do not sell personal information.

## 4. Cross-border transfers

Some of our service providers store or process information outside South Africa. Where this happens, we take steps to ensure that the recipient is subject to laws, binding agreements or corporate rules that provide an adequate level of protection, as required by section 72 of POPIA.

## 5. Security

We use reasonable technical and organisational measures, including encrypted connections (HTTPS), access controls enforced at database level, hashed passwords, restricted administrative access, audit logs of sensitive administrative actions, rate limiting and spam protection. No system is completely secure; if a security compromise affects your personal information, we will notify you and the Information Regulator as required by POPIA.

## 6. Retention

We keep personal information only for as long as needed for the purposes above or as required by law. When you delete your account, we delete your profile and personal account information and remove listings that you were the only owner of. Enquiries you sent to businesses remain with the businesses that received them. Limited records, such as security logs and records of data requests, may be kept for a reasonable period for legal and security purposes.

## 7. Your rights

Subject to POPIA, you have the right to:

- Access the personal information we hold about you — use **Download my data** in your account settings or contact us.
- Request correction or deletion of your personal information — most information can be edited in your dashboard, and you can delete your account at any time.
- Object to processing based on legitimate interests and to direct marketing.
- Withdraw consent at any time (this doesn't affect processing that happened before withdrawal).
- Lodge a complaint with the Information Regulator (South Africa): [inforegulator.org.za](https://inforegulator.org.za).

To exercise your rights, contact our Information Officer at [privacy email address] or use our [contact form](/contact).

## 8. Children

The Platform is not intended for children under 18. We do not knowingly collect personal information from children without the consent of a competent person.

## 9. Changes to this policy

We may update this policy. We will post the updated version on this page and, for material changes, notify registered users by email.
$md$, 'Privacy Policy', 'How Findora collects, uses, shares and protects personal information, and your rights under the Protection of Personal Information Act (POPIA).', true, true) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.site_pages (slug, title, summary, content, seo_title, seo_description, is_published, show_in_footer) values ('terms', 'Terms and Conditions', 'The terms that apply when you use Findora as a customer or as a business.', $md$These terms apply to your use of findora.co.za and related services (the "Platform"), operated by [Findora operating entity name] (Registration number: [number]) ("Findora", "we", "us"). By using the Platform you agree to these terms. If you do not agree, please do not use the Platform.

## 1. What Findora does

Findora is an online directory that helps customers discover businesses and contact them directly. **Findora is not a party to any agreement between a customer and a business**, does not provide the services listed and does not guarantee the quality, safety, legality or pricing of any business, product or service.

## 2. Accounts

- You must provide accurate information and keep it up to date.
- You are responsible for keeping your password confidential and for activity on your account.
- You must be at least 18 years old, or have the consent of a parent or guardian, to create an account.
- We may suspend or close accounts that breach these terms, our listing guidelines or the law.

## 3. Business listings

If you create a listing, you confirm that:

- You own the business or are authorised to represent it.
- All information is accurate, lawful and not misleading.
- You hold any licence, registration or qualification required for the services you offer.
- You have the right to publish the photographs and content you upload.
- You are authorised to publish the contact details provided and consent to their publication.

Listings are reviewed before publication. We may decline, edit for formatting or accuracy, hide content from, suspend or remove listings at our discretion, in particular where they breach our [listing guidelines](/listing-guidelines). You grant Findora a non-exclusive, royalty-free licence to display, reproduce and format your listing content on the Platform and in promotion of the Platform for as long as the listing is published.

You can edit, unpublish or delete your listing at any time from your dashboard.

## 4. Fees

Business listings are currently free. We may introduce optional paid features in future. We will tell you clearly before you incur any charge, and free features will not become paid without notice.

## 5. Customer enquiries

- Enquiries are delivered to the business you select. The business is responsible for responding.
- Businesses must use enquiry information only to respond to the enquiry and provide their services, in line with POPIA.
- Do not use enquiries to send spam, advertising or unlawful content.

## 6. Acceptable use

You may not:

- Post false, misleading, defamatory, offensive or unlawful content.
- Impersonate any person or business.
- Post fake reviews, testimonials or misleading verification claims.
- Scrape, copy or harvest data from the Platform without our written permission.
- Interfere with the security or operation of the Platform.
- Use the Platform to send unsolicited marketing.

## 7. Verification and featured listings

A verification badge means only that Findora completed the verification step described for that badge at the time. It is not an endorsement or guarantee. Featured or sponsored listings are clearly labelled; such labels do not imply endorsement.

## 8. Intellectual property

The Findora name, logo, design and Platform content (excluding user content) belong to Findora or its licensors. You may not use them without permission.

## 9. Disclaimers and limitation of liability

The Platform is provided "as is". To the extent permitted by law, including the Consumer Protection Act where it applies, Findora is not liable for any loss arising from your use of the Platform, from information published by businesses, or from any dealings between customers and businesses. Nothing in these terms limits liability that cannot lawfully be limited.

## 10. Reporting and takedowns

You can report a listing using **Report this listing** on its profile or through our [contact page](/contact). We review reports and act where appropriate.

## 11. Privacy

Our [Privacy Policy](/privacy) and [Cookie Policy](/cookies) explain how we handle personal information.

## 12. Changes

We may update these terms. Material changes will be posted on this page and, where appropriate, communicated by email. Continued use of the Platform after changes means you accept the updated terms.

## 13. Governing law and contact

These terms are governed by the laws of the Republic of South Africa. Contact us at [contact email address] or via our [contact page](/contact).
$md$, 'Terms and Conditions', 'The terms that apply when you use Findora as a customer or as a business.', true, true) on conflict (slug) do nothing;
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'What is Findora?', 'Findora is a South African business discovery platform. Customers can search for businesses and services, compare profiles and contact businesses directly. Businesses can create a free listing to be found by new customers.', 'general', 10, true, true where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'What is Findora?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'Is Findora free?', 'Yes. Searching and contacting businesses is free for customers, and business listings are free. In future we may offer optional premium features for businesses, which will always be clearly explained before any charge.', 'general', 20, true, true where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'Is Findora free?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'Does Findora check the businesses listed?', 'Every listing is reviewed by our team before it is published, and anyone can report a listing. A **verification badge** is only shown when we have completed an additional verification process. A listing on Findora is not an endorsement — always confirm qualifications, prices and terms directly with the business.', 'general', 30, true, false where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'Does Findora check the businesses listed?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'Does Findora publish reviews or ratings?', 'Not at this stage. We will never publish fake reviews or ratings. If we introduce reviews in future, they will come from genuine customers and be clearly moderated.', 'general', 40, true, false where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'Does Findora publish reviews or ratings?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'Do I need an account to contact a business?', 'No. You can call, WhatsApp or send an enquiry without an account. A free account lets you save businesses and see your enquiry history.', 'customers', 50, true, true where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'Do I need an account to contact a business?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'What happens after I send an enquiry?', 'The business receives your enquiry by email and in its dashboard, and replies to you directly using the contact details you provided. You''ll also receive a confirmation email from Findora.', 'customers', 60, true, false where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'What happens after I send an enquiry?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'Who can see my contact details when I send an enquiry?', 'Only the business you contacted. Findora administrators can see limited information about enquiries where it''s needed to prevent abuse. We never publish your contact details or sell your information.', 'customers', 70, true, false where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'Who can see my contact details when I send an enquiry?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'How do I report a suspicious business?', 'Open the business profile and choose **Report this listing**, or use our [report page](/report). Our moderation team reviews every report.', 'customers', 80, true, false where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'How do I report a suspicious business?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'How do I list my business?', '[Register your business](/register/business), verify your email and follow the step-by-step setup: business details, location, contact details and hours, photos and services. Then submit your listing for review.', 'businesses', 90, true, true where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'How do I list my business?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'How long does approval take?', 'Most listings are reviewed within two working days. We''ll email you as soon as your listing is approved, or explain what needs to change if it can''t be approved yet.', 'businesses', 100, true, true where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'How long does approval take?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'Can I manage more than one business?', 'Yes. One account can manage several businesses. Go to **Dashboard → My Businesses → Add a business**.', 'businesses', 110, true, false where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'Can I manage more than one business?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'Do I have to show my prices?', 'No. Each service can be marked as fixed price, starting from, hourly, quotation required or free. Showing ''starting from'' prices helps customers decide to contact you, but it''s your choice.', 'businesses', 120, true, false where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'Do I have to show my prices?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'Can I hide my home address?', 'Yes. Untick **Show my street address publicly** in the location step. You''ll still appear in searches for your town and service areas.', 'businesses', 130, true, false where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'Can I hide my home address?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'Where do the statistics in my dashboard come from?', 'From real visitor activity on your profile: profile views, service views, phone, WhatsApp, email, website and directions clicks, and enquiries. Views are counted once per visitor per day and known bots are excluded.', 'businesses', 140, true, false where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'Where do the statistics in my dashboard come from?');
insert into public.faqs (question, answer, audience, sort_order, is_published, show_on_home) select 'How do I remove my listing?', 'Open your listing in the dashboard and choose **Unpublish listing** to hide it temporarily, or **Delete listing** to remove it.', 'businesses', 150, true, false where not exists (select 1 from public.faqs where question = 'How do I remove my listing?');
